  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 638 PM CDT FRI OCT 2 2026\n\nOverall, we are looking at a cold front to our north and a weak\ntrough to our south. Over the next 48 hours, we will see the\nfront stall out without ever really reaching the area. At the\nsame time, a shortwave disturbance in the Gulf will slide\nnorthward along the trough, developing a low-pressure system as\nit moves. Conceptually, the models are all performing well with\noverall cause and effect; the main challenges lie with model\nprogression and the expected position of the front. Model\nconsensus last week placed the front farther south and the low\nfarther west, with moisture pooling along the front-a setup that\nwould have resulted in copious rainfall for the state. Over the\npast few days, models have trended slower with the front and\nfarther east with the low, essentially cutting off the best\nmoisture from the state and forecast area. Over the last two\nruns, trends favor the front stalling slightly farther north\nwhile the low moves into the Mississippi Delta region. This\nscenario appears reasonable and plausible given climatological\npatterns for stalling fronts this time of year.\n\nSo what does this mean for rainfall potential over the weekend?\nRadar currently shows light echoes working into southern Alabama,\nwith good coverage along the coast as well as the east coast of\nFlorida. These two areas are almost banding around the low to our\nsouthwest. For the rest of the day and tonight, we should only\nsee a modest increase in rain chances to the north, while\nsouthern areas may see decent coverage into the evening.\nOvernight, most activity will retreat back to the south before\nbeginning a slow northward crawl on Saturday. Areas north of I-\n85 will generally see isolated to scattered rain chances during\nthe day on Saturday; while it will not be a complete washout, any\nshowers that do form will likely be efficient rainmakers. Rain\nchances ramp up significantly Saturday night through Sunday as\nthe low swings through the area, bringing the highest totals for\nthe event. Regarding expected accumulation, latest probabilities\nindicate an 80-95% chance that most locations will receive at\nleast half an inch of rain through Sunday night. Probabilities\nfor exceeding 1 inch remain high at 75-90%. However, chances for\n2 inches or more drop to 30-50% in the north and 60-80% in the\nsouth, while probabilities for greater than 3 inches are only 10-\n20% in the north and 30-40% in the south. Overall, there is\nroughly an 80% chance of seeing at least 1 inch of rain across\nthe area, with lower chances for higher totals.\n\nBy Tuesday, a strong trough is expected to slide down into the\narea, clearing the stalled front and ushering in more seasonable\nweather through the end of next week. High temperatures may only\nreach the mid-to-upper 70s and low 80s by this time next week.\n\n16",
